#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:1
msgid "Linking to items by name"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:3
msgid ""
"Rustdoc is capable of directly linking to other rustdoc pages using the path "
"of the item as a link. This is referred to as an 'intra-doc link'."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:6
msgid ""
"For example, in the following code all of the links will link to the rustdoc "
"page for `Bar`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:9
msgid "/// This struct is not [Bar]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:11
msgid "/// This struct is also not [bar](Bar)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:14
msgid ""
"/// This struct is also not [bar][b]\n"
"///\n"
"/// [b]: Bar\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:19
msgid "/// This struct is also not [`Bar`]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:22
msgid "/// This struct *is* [`Bar`]!\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:27
msgid ""
"Unlike normal Markdown, `[bar][Bar]` syntax is also supported without "
"needing a `[Bar]: ...` reference link."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:30
msgid ""
"Backticks around the link will be stripped, so ``[`Option`]`` will correctly "
"link to `Option`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:33
msgid "Valid links"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:35
msgid ""
"You can refer to anything in scope, and use paths, including `Self`, `self`, "
"`super`, and `crate`. Associated items (functions, types, and constants) are "
"supported, but [not for blanket trait implementations](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/rust/pull/79682). Rustdoc also supports linking to all primitives "
"listed in [the standard library documentation](../../std/index."
"html#primitives)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:42
msgid ""
"You can also refer to items with generic parameters like `Vec<T>`. The link "
"will resolve as if you had written ``[`Vec<T>`](Vec)``. Fully-qualified "
"syntax (for example, `<Vec as IntoIterator>::into_iter()`) is [not yet "
"supported](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/74563), however."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:50
msgid ""
"/// This is a version of [`Receiver<T>`] with support for [`std::future`].\n"
"///\n"
"/// You can obtain a [`std::future::Future`] by calling [`Self::recv()`].\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:65
msgid "Rustdoc allows using URL fragment specifiers, just like a normal link:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:68
msgid ""
"/// This is a special implementation of [positional parameters].\n"
"///\n"
"/// [positional parameters]: std::fmt#formatting-parameters\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:74
msgid "Namespaces and Disambiguators"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:76
msgid ""
"Paths in Rust have three namespaces: type, value, and macro. Item names must "
"be unique within their namespace, but can overlap with items in other "
"namespaces. In case of ambiguity, rustdoc will warn about the ambiguity and "
"suggest a disambiguator."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:81
msgid "/// See also: [`Foo`](struct@Foo)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:83
msgid "/// This is different from [`Foo`](fn@Foo)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:90
msgid ""
"These prefixes will be stripped when displayed in the documentation, so "
"`[struct@Foo]` will be rendered as `Foo`. The following prefixes are "
"available: `struct`, `enum`, `trait`, `union`, `mod`, `module`, `const`, "
"`constant`, `fn`, `function`, `field`, `variant`, `method`, `derive`, "
"`type`, `value`, `macro`, `tyalias`, `typealias`, `prim` or `primitive`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:95
msgid ""
"You can also disambiguate for functions by adding `()` after the function "
"name, or for macros by adding `!` after the macro name. The macro `!` can be "
"followed by `()`, `{}`, or `[]`. Example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:100
msgid "/// This is different from [`foo!()`].\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:102
msgid "/// This is different from [`foo()`]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:109
msgid ""
"There is one case where the disambiguation will be performed automatically: "
"if an intra doc link is resolved at the same time as a trait and as a derive "
"proc-macro. In this case, it'll always generate a link to the trait and not "
"emit a \"missing disambiguation\" warning. A good example of this case is "
"when you link to the `Clone` trait: there is also a `Clone` proc-macro but "
"it ignores it in this case. If you want to link to the proc-macro, you can "
"use the `macro@` disambiguator."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:116
msgid "Warnings, re-exports, and scoping"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:118
msgid ""
"Links are resolved in the scope of the module where the item is defined, "
"even when the item is re-exported. If a link from another crate fails to "
"resolve, no warning is given."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:124
msgid "/// Link to [f()]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:128
msgid "// the link to `f` will still resolve correctly\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:131
msgid ""
"When re-exporting an item, rustdoc allows adding additional documentation to "
"it. That additional documentation will be resolved in the scope of the re-"
"export, not the original, allowing you to link to items in the new crate. "
"The new links will still give a warning if they fail to resolve."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:137
msgid "/// See also [foo()]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:143
msgid ""
"This is especially useful for proc-macros, which must always be defined in "
"their own dedicated crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:145
msgid ""
"Note: Because of how `macro_rules!` macros are scoped in Rust, the intra-doc "
"links of a `macro_rules!` macro will be resolved [relative to the crate root]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/72243), as opposed to the module "
"it is defined in."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:149
msgid ""
"If links do not look 'sufficiently like' an intra-doc link, they will be "
"ignored and no warning will be given, even if the link fails to resolve. For "
"example, any link containing `/` or `[]` characters will be ignored."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:155
msgid "What happens in case an intra-doc link cannot be generated"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:157
msgid ""
"In some cases (items behind a `cfg` for example), an intra-doc link cannot "
"be generated to item. There are different ways to create a link in markdown, "
"and depending on the one you use, it will render differently in this case:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:170
msgid ""
"`1.` and `2.` will be displayed as is in the rendered documentation (ie, "
"`[a]` and `[b][c]`) whereas `3.` and `4.` will be replaced by a link "
"targeting `e` for `[d](e)` and `g` for `[f]`."
msgstr ""
